- Compare branchless popcount against using CPU intrinsics.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless thinking and lane-wise masking, fundamental techniques in low-level libraries and SIMD operations.

## Key Concepts
- Lane-wise masking without conditionals using two's complement arithmetic
- 128-bit composition from 32-bit chunks
- Bitwise extraction and packing operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode C-like implementation demonstrating the core idea
uint128 blendMask128(uint128 x){
  uint32_t a3 = (x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a2 = x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a1 = (x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a0 = x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint64_t m3 = -(uint64_t)(a3 & 1);
  uint64_t m2 = -(uint64_t)(a2 & 1);
  uint64_t m1 = -(uint64_t)(a1 & 1);
  uint64_t m0 = -(uint64_t)(a0 & 1);
  uint128 result;
  result.hi = (m3 << 32) | m2;
  result.lo = (m1 << 32) | m0;
  return result;
}
```

## Quick Tests
- Input: 0x123456789ABCDEF0 → Output: 0x0000000000000000 (all lanes even)
- Input: 0x12345679_9ABCDEF1 → Output: 0xFFFFFFFF_00000000 (only A0 odd)

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes low-level bit-tracking across a 128-bit value, edge cases at zero, and portable handling when single-word intrinsics are unavailable. It mirrors real-world tasks where bitmaps or flags span multiple words.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit split into two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count logic with early non-zero word optimizations
- Portable fallback for environments lacking 64-bit ctzn intrinsics
- Edge case for zero input

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  if (x.lo) return __builtin_ctzll(x.lo);
  if (x.hi) return 64 + __builtin_ctzll(x.hi);
  return 128;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to implement without __builtin_ctzll?
- How would you adapt for architectures without 64-bit support?
- Extend to 256-bit using a similar two-word strategy and discuss performance implications.

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Could you implement a pure bitwise variant that does not rely on reverse128?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values with four halves?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a branchless bit-packing operation across 128 bits—a common low-level optimization used in compression algorithms, bitmap libraries, and SIMD data processing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit masks and shifts
- Branchless algorithms
- Parallel bit extraction and compression

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 compress128(uint128 x, uint128 mask) {
    // Stage 1: compact adjacent bits (shift 1)
    uint128 m = mask & ~(mask << 1);
    x = (x & m) | ((x >> 1) & ~m);
    mask &= ~(mask << 1);

    // Subsequent stages (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64) follow the same pattern
    // with appropriate shift amounts...

    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compose 128-bit reversal from 64-bit primitives without branching.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit bit reversal algorithm
- Cross-block mapping for 128-bit reversal
- Constant-time, no-branch implementation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo_rev = rev64(x.lo);
  uint64_t hi_rev = rev64(x.hi);
  return (uint128){ .hi = lo_rev, .lo = hi_rev };
}
```

